(roughly bottom to top of the distribution), or a number of units (\code{10} = per decade of age);
\code{multiplier = 1} restores the per-one-unit reading. The row's level states every fact that
places it --- the transform \code{shape} fitted it through, the unit its effect is per, and the \code{ref}
anchor the Constant row sits at: \verb{log(x), per 0.39 (SD), at 3.78 (mean)}. The variable itself is
named in the \code{var} column beside it.

\code{multiplier}, \code{shape} and \code{ref} share one grammar: a \strong{value on its own} applies to every
continuous predictor, a \strong{named} one overrides that variable, and \verb{default =} spells the first
out --- so \code{multiplier = c("2sd", age = 10)} reads "per two standard deviations, except \code{age},
per decade".

Everything scales together --- the estimate, its interval, the crude \verb{Obs_*} companion and the
model-versus-observed comparison; the p-value is unchanged. \strong{Because the default is not 1, a
continuous predictor's \verb{Model_*} cell does not equal \code{exp(coef(glm(...)))} unless you pass
\code{multiplier = 1}.} The standard deviation is measured \strong{once}, on the complete cases of the
predictors, so one predictor keeps one unit across outcomes, compared models and \code{tab_vars}
groups. Applied to every family, multinomial and ordinal included; never to a \code{formula} model
(it would scale the main effect alone). A 0/1-coded \strong{numeric} predictor gets a \verb{per SD/0.5}
reading --- pass it as a factor instead.}

\item{shape}{How a \strong{continuous} predictor enters the model, when one straight line is not
enough. The \code{Linearity} footer row and the little curve drawn in the predictor's \code{n} cell tell
you \emph{whether} a line is enough; this argument is how you fix it without leaving the framework.
Same grammar as \code{multiplier} and \code{ref} --- \code{shape = "quintiles"} cuts every continuous
predictor, \code{shape = c(age = "quadratic")} only that one, and anything unnamed stays linear:
\describe{
\item{\code{"linear"}}{one slope (the default).}
\item{\code{"quintiles"} / \code{"quartiles"} / an integer \code{k}}{cut into \code{k} quantile groups, so the
predictor becomes an ordinary \strong{factor}: one estimate per group, its own observed
companion, counts and colours per group --- the non-linearity becomes visible in the printed
numbers. Start here; it is the most readable answer.}
\item{\code{"sd_bands"}}{cut at the \strong{mean and one standard deviation either side} --- four
bands, whose labels carry both the real cut points and the cut each band names
(\verb{[30,47) ; < mean}), so a reader can check one against the other. It is the classic
low / average / high reading of moderated
regression, and its cut points mean the same thing across sub-samples of one variable, where
quantiles move with each one. \emph{Unlike quantile groups the bands are not balanced}: on a
skewed variable the bottom one can be small, and a landmark falling outside the data is
dropped (an exponential variable gets three bands, not four). Prefer quantiles when the
group sizes matter.}
\item{\code{"quadratic"}}{adds a curvature term, so the predictor takes \strong{two rows} --- the slope
at the mean, and the squared term, which says whether the slope flattens or accelerates as
you move away from it.}
\item{\code{"log"} / \code{"sqrt"}}{fit \code{log(x)} / \code{sqrt(x)} instead of \code{x} --- diminishing returns. The
row label says which (\code{log(age)}); \code{"log"} needs strictly positive values.}
}
Example: \code{shape = c(age = "quadratic", income = "log")}. Everything else keeps working: the
observed companion is fitted with the same shape, the comparisons compare like with like, and
\code{multiplier} still names the unit. A \code{poly()} / \code{ns()} basis is deliberately never emitted ---
the marginal-effects engine silently returns zero for those.
It is the same vocabulary \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}} takes, on one vector in \code{\link[=shape_numeric_var]{shape_numeric_var()}} --- minus
\code{"quadratic"}, which is a model term and so belongs to this function alone.}

\item{stats}{The statistics shown in the model-summary \strong{footer} (one block per model). \code{NULL}
(default) uses the per-family set: linear models show R square, adjusted R square, the
overall F-test and the residual SD; other models show the likelihood-ratio test versus the
null model, McFadden's pseudo-R square, AIC and BIC (count and grouped-binomial models also show
the Pearson dispersion, \code{"phi"}). Every default set also carries the \strong{default model checks}
(see below). Pass a character vector to
pick the statistics (\code{"n"}, \code{"lr_null"}, \code{"mcfadden_r2"}, \code{"aic"}, \code{"bic"}, \code{"phi"}, \code{"r2"},
\code{"r2_adj"}, \code{"f_model"}, \code{"sigma"}, \code{"global"}, \code{"interaction"}, \code{"group_interaction"},
\code{"linearity"},
\code{"proportionality"}, \code{"dispersion"}, \code{"influence"}, \code{"collinearity"}), \code{"all"} for everything
this model can report, or \code{FALSE} / \code{"none"} to hide the footer. Weighted models show a reduced,
survey-appropriate set (design-based Wald test, Nagelkerke pseudo-R square, AIC).

\strong{Model comparison happens by default} wherever it means anything --- when \code{predictors} is a
list of several models. Which one it is, is read off the models: where each nests in the next
(a chain a reader builds on purpose) every model is tested against the \strong{previous} one,
otherwise each against the \strong{first}. Both use a likelihood-ratio test (F for linear / quasi
models, a design-based Wald test for weighted ones), falling back to the AIC difference where
the models are not nested or have different N.

Two keys override it: \code{"compare_sequential"}, and \code{"compare_baseline"} --- against the first
model, or the one you name as the key's value, \code{stats = c("n", "aic", compare_baseline = "Model 1")}. A comparison key \strong{adds} a row and restricts nothing. And naming any footer
statistic drops the automatic comparison, the way naming any argument's values drops the rest:
\code{stats = c("n", "aic")} is a footer with no comparison in it.

\code{"global"} adds one \strong{overall test per predictor} --- "is this variable associated with the
outcome at all?", the question a block of stars against a reference category cannot answer.
Ask for it by name: it is one row per multi-level predictor and a refit each, which on a table
whose every block is strongly associated only repeats what the stars already said.
\code{"interaction"} tests each \strong{crossed pair} written in \code{predictors} (\code{race:age4}): is the
interaction real, or is the additive model enough? It compares the two models directly, so it
costs one extra fit --- about 20 ms on a linear or logistic model, which is why it is already in
the default set there, and roughly a doubling of the fitting time on a multinomial or ordinal
outcome, where you ask for it by name. It produces nothing unless \code{predictors} declares a pair.
\code{"group_interaction"} is the other question: it needs \code{tab_vars} and adds one \strong{aggregated
effect-modification test per predictor}, asked once for all its levels together, so it carries
none of the multiplicity of the per-cell \code{color = "between_groups"} colours --- which turns it
on for you. It also costs one extra fit, and is not available for multinomial or ordinal
outcomes (nor is \code{"global"}).

The footer reads \strong{outward from the data}: the N first, then the checks --- worst first, since
proportionality and linearity break what the number \emph{means} while dispersion breaks every star
in the table and collinearity and influence only say how fragile it is --- then what the
table's own rows say jointly, then how good the model is and against what.}

\item{conf_level}{Confidence level for the intervals. Default \code{0.95}. It drives every interval in
the table, the significance stars, the greying under \code{color_signif} and the
model-versus-observed gap interval, and is stored on each column, so it follows this argument
rather than \code{options("tabxplor.conf_level")}.}

\item{na}{Which rows each model is fitted on --- the grain at which missing values are dropped.
\code{"drop_by_outcome"} (default) gives every model \strong{of one outcome} the same complete-case
population (no \code{NA} on the outcome, on any predictor of any model in the call, or on a design
variable); a second outcome keeps its own rows. That is what makes the comparisons honest: the
observed columns are computed on exactly the model's rows, and nested models get equal N so the
likelihood-ratio comparison can run instead of degrading to an AIC difference.
\code{"drop_by_model"} lets each model use its own complete cases --- more rows, at the price of
comparability: models fitted on different people get no observed effect at all. \code{"drop_all"}
shares one population across the whole call, all outcomes included.}

\item{display}{What each effect cell shows --- \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}}'s display grammar, same names, same
meaning, on every family and on the crude column as well as the model one. \code{NULL} (default)
shows the plain estimate, or, with \code{empirical}, the estimate with the level it sits on beside
it. The named layouts:
\itemize{
\item \code{"est"} --- the effect alone.
\item \code{"est_ci"} --- with a visible interval: \verb{1/2.22*** [1/2.47;1/1.99]}. The stars and the colour
stay on the effect; the bracket is the aside.
\item \code{"est_base"} --- the effect with the level beside it: \verb{1/2.22 (32.8\%)} on a logistic model,
\code{-0.89 (2.25)} on a linear one. On a model column that level is the \strong{adjusted} prediction;
on a crude column the observed percentage or mean.
\item \code{"base_est"} --- the mirror, level first: \verb{(32.8\%) 1/2.22}. The effect stays the number the
cell is about (it carries the stars and the colour); the bracket is the aside.
\item \code{"est_coef"} --- the effect with the model's own coefficient beside it: \code{1/2.22 (-0.80)}, the
logarithm on a ratio scale, the effect itself on an additive one.
\item \code{"base_est_mdiff"} / \code{"base_est_mratio"} --- \strong{the same comparison read the other way}:
\verb{1/2.22 (-22\%)} puts the effect in percentage points beside an odds ratio,
\verb{1/2.22 (\\u00f71.78)} as a ratio. The observed column keeps its level, \verb{(31\%) 1/2.68}, so the
two effects stay side by side. Where the column already \emph{is} that geometry --- a risk
difference under \code{"base_est_mdiff"} --- the aside would repeat the number and is dropped.
\item \code{"base"} --- the levels alone, still coloured and starred by the effect.
}

A cell that cannot fill an aside leaves it \strong{blank but padded}, so the effects still line up;
an aside no cell of the column can fill is dropped, padding and all.

The \strong{Constant} row is the baseline the rest of the column is read against, at the very point
that column's effects are read at: under \code{effect = "conditional"} the model's intercept ---
every factor at its reference level and every continuous predictor at its \code{ref} anchor, the
mean by default; under \code{"at_reference"} the outcome the model predicts at that same profile;
under \code{"marginal"} the \strong{population average} the sample-averaged effects sit on, which is what
its label then says.

It holds \strong{the quantity the column's effects operate on}, so it is read in one step: an odds
ratio multiplies odds, so an odds column shows the baseline \emph{odds} (\code{1/1.51}) with the
probability beside it (\verb{(40\%)}); a risk or rate ratio multiplies the level and a difference adds
to it, so those show the \strong{level itself} --- \verb{39\%}, \code{40.8}, \code{2.9}; and \code{measure = "coefficient"} shows
the intercept on the link scale. It is a baseline, not a comparison, so it never carries a \code{+}
or a \code{x} sign. It carries its confidence interval, and a \strong{star} only where what it prints has
a null to be tested against (the odds and link scales, under \code{effect = "conditional"}): a
predicted 48.7 \% is not "different from zero". An ordinal outcome leaves it empty (a cumulative
logit has thresholds, not one intercept).

Or write a \code{{}} template: \code{"{est} (obs {obs})"} prints each adjusted effect next to the
unadjusted one, \code{"{est} ({gap})"} next to how far adjustment moved it.

\code{display} is a \strong{post-hoc} property: every quantity it can name is already stored, so choosing
a layout never triggers a computation and never changes a number --- \code{\link[=set_display]{set_display()}} on a built
table gives the same result as asking for it here. It never changes the fit or the estimand,
which is \code{measure}'s job alone.}

\item{cleannames}{Logical. If \code{TRUE}, strips numeric prefixes from factor levels for display.
Uses \code{getOption("tabxplor.cleannames")} when \code{NULL}.}

\item{subtext}{Optional character. A note shown below the table.}

\item{...}{One rarely-typed argument, plus internal plumbing.

\code{ci_method} --- how the interval and p-value are computed: the same argument, and the same
named-vector grammar, as in \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}}, whose fifth slot is this producer's:
\code{ci_method = c(model = "profile")}. On a regression there is only one interval to choose a
method for, so a bare \code{"profile"} means that slot. \code{"wald"} (default) uses the Wald interval and
the Wald z / t test: fast, matching standard software output, and the only option for weighted
models. \code{"profile"} uses the profile-likelihood interval (\code{\link[stats:confint]{stats::confint()}}, needs \code{MASS}) and
the likelihood-ratio test: more accurate near separation, unweighted binomial / poisson only
(otherwise it falls back to Wald with a message; gaussian always uses the exact-t interval).

\code{...} also carries the internal \code{.fit_cache} (the jamovi live UI's fit
cache environment), and it is what makes every argument removed or renamed while \code{tab_reg()} was
in development --- \code{exponentiate}, \code{at}, \code{estimate_display}, \code{dependent}, \code{split_var},
\code{reference}, \code{method}, \code{compare}, \code{baseline}, \code{inverse_two_level_factors}, \code{parallel}, and the
\code{effect} values \code{"ame"} / \code{"ame_ratio"} / \code{"coefficient"} --- give an error naming its
replacement, rather than R's bare "unused argument".}
}
\value{
A \code{tabxplor_grouped_tab} (grouped by predictor), one effect column per model / outcome.
}
\description{
Fits one regression model per column and returns a \code{tabxplor} table of the per-family effect
measure --- a linear \strong{mean difference} (gaussian), \strong{odds ratios} (binomial / logistic),
\strong{incidence-rate ratios} (poisson), one \strong{odds-ratio column per outcome category} (nominal 3+
level), a \strong{cumulative odds ratio} (ordinal) --- with one row per predictor level, the reference
level shown as the neutral value \code{0} or \code{1}, grouped by predictor. Each cell stores its estimate,
confidence interval and p-value, so the table prints with significance stars, greys out what is
not significant, and exports (HTML / Markdown / Excel) like any \code{tabxplor} crosstab.
}
\details{
New to regressions with tabxplor? A first model needs three arguments: \code{data}, \code{outcome} and
\code{predictors}. The model follows the outcome's type --- a two-level factor gives logistic \strong{odds
ratios}, a numeric a linear \strong{mean difference}, a count Poisson \strong{rate ratios}, a 3+ level
factor multinomial or ordinal odds ratios --- so you rarely set \code{family} by hand. Add
\code{empirical = TRUE} to put the crude (unadjusted) effect beside each adjusted one. See
\code{vignette("tabxplor-reg")} for a guided tour.

\strong{The estimand is a cascade}: \code{family} -> \code{link} -> \code{measure} -> \code{effect}, where \code{"auto"} means
\emph{follow from the left}. \code{family} says what kind of number the outcome is; \code{link} \strong{which measure
the model estimates} (a link \emph{is} a measure, so it takes the same words); \code{measure} \strong{which one
is reported}; \code{effect} where that number comes from. Set any one and everything to its right
re-derives, so most tables set none of them --- and the one most people ever set is \code{measure}.

A \strong{coefficient} exists only where the reported measure IS the model's own. Ask for another and
it is worked out from the model's predictions instead, averaged over the sample. So
\code{measure = "ratio"} on a binary outcome gives a \emph{marginal} risk ratio from the logistic fit, while
\code{link = "ratio"} fits the modified Poisson and gives its \emph{conditional} one --- two different
quantities, and now two different arguments.

The arguments fall into groups:
\itemize{
\item \strong{The model}: \code{data}, \code{outcome}, \code{predictors} (a character vector = one model, a named
list = several models to compare), \code{family}, \code{link}, \code{wt} (survey weights).
\item \strong{What each cell shows}: \code{measure} x \code{effect} (which measure, where it comes from),
\code{display} (the layout), \code{empirical} (the crude effect beside the adjusted one).
\item \strong{Colours & significance}: \code{color}, \code{color_signif}, \code{stars}, \code{conf_level}, as in \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}}.
\item \strong{Comparisons & structure}: \code{ref} (baseline levels), \code{outcome_level} (the level
modelled), \code{tab_vars} (one table per group), \code{multiplier} (a continuous predictor's unit).
\item \strong{The footer}: \code{stats} --- goodness-of-fit rows, model checks and the model comparison.
\item \strong{Fixing what a check flags}: \code{shape}, drawn by \code{\link[=reg_check_plots]{reg_check_plots()}}. \strong{Charting the
result}: \code{\link[=forest_plot]{forest_plot()}}.
}

\code{predictors} selects the mode: a \strong{character vector} fits one model, and \code{outcome} may then be a
vector too (one column per outcome); a \strong{named list} of predictor sets fits one model each, one
column per model, for comparing specifications (a predictor absent from a model leaves its cells
blank).
}
\section{Model checks}{


Five checks, in the order of what each one threatens --- the estimate, what the estimate means,
its interval, whether it is real at all, and why it is wide. Each is a footer row, so it travels
into every export, and each is named in \code{stats}.

Four of them are \strong{shown by default}: dispersion, influence, collinearity, and --- on an ordinal
outcome --- proportionality, which is a refit and a default anyway, because a cumulative odds
ratio that fails it is not one number but a fiction. \strong{Linearity} is the one you ask for by
name (\code{stats = "linearity"}), or draw with \code{\link[=reg_check_plots]{reg_check_plots()}}, which shows the shape instead of
scoring it.

\describe{
\item{\strong{Linearity} (p-value, per numeric predictor)}{Is this predictor's effect really one
straight line? The model is refitted with that predictor's centred squared term and the two
compared. The damage a curve does is \strong{not confined to its own row}. Cure it with \code{shape}.}
\item{\strong{Proportionality (Brant)} (p-value, ordinal outcomes)}{Is one cumulative odds ratio
enough for every cut of the outcome? Read it beside the size of the departure: at survey
sample sizes it rejects on differences the eye calls mild. Weighted ordinal models have no
Brant fit, so the row is absent rather than approximated.}
\item{\strong{Dispersion (robust/model SE)} (a ratio)}{Are the standard errors wide enough? The
largest ratio of a robust (sandwich) standard error to the model-based one. About 1 means the
family's variance assumption holds; above 1 it does not --- over-dispersion,
heteroscedasticity or clustering, by roughly that factor.}
\item{\strong{Influence (max dfbetas)} (a ratio)}{Does one respondent carry the result? The largest
change dropping a single observation makes to a coefficient, in units of its own standard
error. Printed as a \emph{reassurance}: with thousands of respondents a near-zero value is the
finding. Influence is not outlyingness. \strong{Marked} from 1 --- one respondent moving a
coefficient by a whole standard error.}
\item{\strong{Collinearity (max VIF)} (a ratio)}{Can the data tell these predictors apart? The
largest variance inflation factor (\code{car::vif()}). The one check that is not a comparison with
the data --- collinearity biases nothing, it only widens intervals. \strong{Marked} from 10, the
textbook figure. Needs \code{car}; refused for multinomial outcomes.}
}

The two marked thresholds are \strong{conventions, not tests}: no cut-off on a variance inflation
factor or a dfbeta has a null distribution behind it, and the literature is openly divided on the
VIF one. So a marked cell wears the \emph{faintest} shade, a warning --- "look at this" --- where a
non-significant test wears the deepest. Read the number, not the mark.

Dispersion, Influence and Collinearity are arithmetic on the model already fitted, so they ride
the default footer and cost nothing; \strong{Proportionality} fits J-1 binary logits and is a default
anyway on an ordinal outcome, because a cumulative odds ratio that fails it is not one number but
a fiction. \strong{Linearity} is the one asked for by name (\code{stats = c("n", "aic", "linearity")}, or
\code{stats = "all"}). The cheap answer is on screen either way: each numeric predictor's observed
shape is binned with no fit at all and drawn as a \strong{sparkline}, always beside the \strong{observed
range} it is a picture of --- \verb{13-57\% (OR 8.7)}: the curve's lowest and highest point, back on
the outcome's own scale, with the climb between them in the measure the \emph{link} estimates. The
picture answers \emph{what shape}, the range answers \emph{how big}, and a curve smaller than its own
sampling noise is greyed and marked \code{ns} --- read that one as a flat line whatever it looks like.
Both go in the predictor's own \code{n} cell where the table has one outcome and the medium can hold
them, and otherwise in a small \strong{shape table} below the footer.

⚠ an \strong{ordinal} or \strong{multinomial} outcome has one curve per cut or per category and the
sparkline draws only the first: use \code{\link[=reg_check_plots]{reg_check_plots()}} there, which shows them all --- and which
draws the full panel for \strong{every} check.
At survey sample sizes a diagnostic p-value rejects almost anything, which is why three of the
five report a \emph{magnitude} instead.
}

\section{Out of scope}{

\code{tab_reg()} covers linear, logistic, Poisson, multinomial and ordinal models, with survey designs.
Three families of models are deliberately \strong{not} supported, and are unlikely to be: \strong{survival /
Cox} models, \strong{mixed / multilevel} models, and pooling over \strong{multiply-imputed} datasets. Fit
those with their own packages.
}

\section{Display layouts}{

The named layouts \code{display} accepts. They are spelt with the scale-relative
\code{\{est\}} / \code{\{base\}} fields, so one name means the same thing on a crosstab
and on a \code{\link{tab_reg}} table:
\itemize{
\item \code{"est"} (\code{\{est\}}) --- the estimate alone.
\item \code{"est_ci"} (\code{\{est\} \{ci\}}) --- the estimate with its confidence interval.
\item \code{"est_base"} (\code{\{est\} (\{base\})}) --- the estimate and, in parentheses, the level it sits on.
\item \code{"est_coef"} (\code{\{est\} (\{coef\})}) --- the estimate and, in parentheses, the model's own coefficient.
\item \code{"base_est_mdiff"} (\code{\{est\} (\{diff\})}) --- the estimate and, in parentheses, the same comparison as a difference.
\item \code{"base_est_mratio"} (\code{\{est\} (\{ratio\})}) --- the estimate and, in parentheses, the same comparison as a ratio.
\item \code{"est_obs"} (\code{(\{obs\}) \{est\}}) --- the estimate and, before it in parentheses, the observed (crude) effect it is compared to.
\item \code{"base_est"} (\code{(\{base\}) \{est\}}) --- the level, then the estimate --- the mirror of \code{est_base}, which sets a crude and a modelled effect side by side.
\item \code{"base"} (\code{\{base\}}) --- the level alone: the percentage, the mean or the count.
\item \code{"base_ci"} (\code{\{base\} \{ci\}}) --- the level with its confidence interval.
\item \code{"base_moe"} (\code{\{base\} \{moe\}}) --- the level with its margin of error.
\item \code{"base_ratio"} (\code{\{base\} (\{ratio\})}) --- the level and, in parentheses, its ratio to the reference.
\item \code{"base_or"} (\code{\{base\} (\{or\})}) --- the level and, in parentheses, its odds ratio.
\item \code{"or_base"} (\code{\{or\} (\{base\})}) --- the odds ratio and, in parentheses, the percentage it rests on.
\item \code{"mean_sd"} (\code{\{mean\} (σ\{sd\})}) --- the mean and, in parentheses, its standard deviation.
\item \code{"mean_cv"} (\code{\{mean\} (cv \{cv\})}) --- the mean and, in parentheses, its coefficient of variation --- the spread as a percentage of the mean, comparable between columns measured in different units (the default where every mean is positive).
}
}

\section{The header acronyms}{

A column header names the \strong{measure}; the \strong{contrast} is a marker on it ---
no marker for a conditional effect, \code{m} for a marginal one, \code{ref} at the reference
profile --- and \code{measure = "log"} wraps it (\code{Model_mRR}, \code{Model_refRD},
\code{Model_log(OR)}). The observed companion carries the measure alone (\code{Obs_RR}).
\itemize{
\item \code{OR} --- odds ratio (binomial, multinomial)
\item \code{cumOR} --- cumulative odds ratio (ordinal)
\item \code{RR} --- risk ratio (binomial, multinomial)
\item \code{RD} --- risk difference (binomial, multinomial)
\item \code{IRR} --- incidence-rate ratio (poisson)
\item \code{RoM} --- ratio of means (gaussian)
\item \code{diff} --- mean difference (gaussian, poisson)
\item \code{D} --- Somers' D (ordinal)
\item \code{WR} --- win ratio (ordinal)
}
}

\section{Which models each outcome offers, and which measures}{

Generated from the package's own resolution table, so it cannot drift from what
\code{tab_reg()} builds. A measure that IS the model's own is read off its coefficients;
any other is computed from its predictions (\code{effect = "marginal"} or
\code{"at_reference"}). Call \code{\link{reg_measures}()} on your outcome for the same
table with its per-cell status.
\itemize{
\item \strong{gaussian} --- a cell is a mean. Models: \code{link = "difference"}, the default (linear regression); \code{link = "ratio"} (log-link mean regression (Poisson pseudo-likelihood, robust standard errors)). Reported: \code{measure = }\code{"difference"}, \code{"ratio"}, \code{"coefficient"}.
\item \strong{binomial} --- a cell is a percentage. Models: \code{link = "odds_ratio"}, the default (logistic regression); \code{link = "ratio"} (modified Poisson regression); \code{link = "difference"} (additive-risk regression (identity link, robust standard errors)). Reported: \code{measure = }\code{"ratio"}, \code{"difference"}, \code{"odds_ratio"}, \code{"coefficient"}.
\item \strong{poisson} --- a cell is a count. Models: \code{link = "ratio"}, the default (Poisson regression). Reported: \code{measure = }\code{"difference"}, \code{"ratio"}, \code{"coefficient"}.
\item \strong{multinomial} --- a cell is a percentage. Models: \code{link = "odds_ratio"}, the default (multinomial logistic regression). Reported: \code{measure = }\code{"ratio"}, \code{"difference"}, \code{"odds_ratio"}, \code{"coefficient"}.
\item \strong{ordinal} --- a cell is a position on an ordered scale. Models: \code{link = "odds_ratio"}, the default (ordinal logistic regression). Reported: \code{measure = }\code{"difference"}, \code{"ratio"}, \code{"odds_ratio"}, \code{"coefficient"}.
}
}

\examples{
  data <- gss_cat_data_formatting()
  # a subset keeps the examples fast: fitting these models on all 21,483 rows costs well over
  # CRAN's 5-second-per-topic budget. Use the full `data` in real analyses.
  reg_data <- head(data, 3000)

  # logistic (odds ratios):
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "married", predictors = c("race", "rincome"),
          family = "binomial")

\donttest{
  # linear (mean differences), and the same model written as a formula:
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "tvhours", predictors = c("rincome", "age"), family = "gaussian")
  tab_reg(reg_data, married ~ race + rincome, family = "binomial")

  # the observed odds ratio beside the modelled one:
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "married", predictors = c("race", "rincome"),
          family = "binomial", empirical = TRUE)

# average marginal effects, as a RATIO: with a common outcome this is what a reader means by
# "x times more likely" -- an odds ratio is not. (Needs the marginaleffects package.)
if (requireNamespace("marginaleffects", quietly = TRUE)) {
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "married", predictors = c("race", "rincome"),
                family = "binomial", effect = "marginal", measure = "ratio", empirical = TRUE)
}
# the CONDITIONAL risk ratio: measure = "ratio" on a binary outcome fits the modified Poisson
# (Zou 2004), a log link with robust standard errors. Ask for the measure, not the distribution.
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "married", predictors = c("race", "rincome"),
                measure = "ratio", empirical = TRUE)
# multinomial (nominal 3+ level): one OR column per outcome category vs the baseline
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "party3", predictors = c("race", "age"),
                family = "multinomial", outcome_level = c(party3 = "3-Republican"))
# ordinal (proportional-odds): one cumulative-OR column
  tab_reg(reg_data, outcome = "rincome", predictors = c("race", "age"), family = "ordinal")
}

}
\references{
Clogg, C. C., Petkova, E. & Haritou, A. (1995). Statistical Methods for Comparing Regression
Coefficients between Models. \emph{American Journal of Sociology}, 100(5), 1261-1293 (with
Allison, P. D. (1995), \emph{ibid.} 1294-1305) --- the comparison \code{color = "adjustment"} implements.

Karlson, K. B., Holm, A. & Breen, R. (2012). Comparing Regression Coefficients Between Same-sample
Nested Models Using Logit and Probit. \emph{Sociological Methodology}, 42(1), 286-313 --- the KHB
decomposition, separating confounding from rescaling in nested logit models.

Zou, G. (2004). A Modified Poisson Regression Approach to Prospective Studies with Binary Data.
\emph{American Journal of Epidemiology}, 159(7), 702-706.

Altman, D. G. & Bland, J. M. (2003). Interaction revisited: the difference between two estimates.
\emph{BMJ}, 326, 219 --- the \code{color = "between_groups"} test.

Santos Silva, J. M. C. & Tenreyro, S. (2006). The log of gravity. \emph{The Review of Economics and
Statistics}, 88(4), 641-658 --- \code{measure = "ratio"} on a continuous outcome.
}
